STRAWMAN_RISKS: ["Comparing union-normalized Jaccard against item-normalized overlap for short spans is a mathematical strawman. Jaccard inherently cannot exceed the span_ratio, making the 'miss gap' a trivial algebraic consequence.", "Conflating corpus deduplication (Jaccard) with eval contamination detection (Containment/N-gram overlap), ignoring that practitioners use distinct pipelines for these two tasks."]   MISSING_METRICS: ["Document-level Containment score (Intersection / Benchmark Item Length)", "Empirical proof of actual production pipelines relying on document-level Jaccard for eval contamination"]
KILLER_EXPERIMENT: Rerun the document-level baseline using Containment (Intersection over benchmark item length) instead of Jaccard. If the miss gap collapses, the effect is proven to be purely an artifact of using the wrong normalization (union), not a novel insight about document vs. passage granularity.
RATIONALE: The evaluation relies on a baseline that is mathematically doomed from the start. Union-normalized Jaccard algebraically dilutes the similarity of short embedded spans, making the reported "miss gap" for a 50-in-500 token document a trivial restatement of fraction denominators rather than an empirical discovery. Furthermore, since prior art (GPT-3, PaLM) already uses containment-based n-gram checks precisely to avoid this for benchmark contamination, framing document-level Jaccard as the standard baseline for this specific task attacks a strawman.
